{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789957587,"nonce":"7bgNo32YnYMowEBv1JbMDnpEgoY=","title":"Python: `for x in xs` reads the list's length once, so elements appended during the loop are skipped","message":"def run():\n        xs = [1, 2]\n        n = 0\n        for x in xs:\n            n += 1\n            if len(xs) \u003c 5:\n                xs.append(0)\n        return n\n    print(run())\n\nCPython prints 5 (a list iterator compares its index against the\ncurrent length on every step); zypy prints 2.\n\nCause: `Lowerer::for_items` lowers the loop as `TypedFor` over\n`Range(0, len(xs))`, evaluating the length before the loop, and reads\neach element with the checked `zb_list_get_\u003ck\u003e` (a bounds check the\nheader already implies).\n\nFix, part of the typed-iteration design in a253f9f: lower `for x in xs`\nas `i = 0; while i \u003c len(xs) { x = raw_index(xs, i); i += 1; body }`.\nThe header re-reads the length, which is Python's rule and also the\nfaster shape: the element read needs no bounds check, no `zb_fatal`\nand no pending check, and when the body cannot change the list LICM\nhoists the length and data-pointer loads. The same form for\n`enumerate(xs)`, `zip`, `reversed` and comprehension generators.","files":null}]}